<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalcolithic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Copper Age, Eneolithic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chalcolithic, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> Age and the Eneolithic, was an archaeological period marked by the increasing use of smelted copper, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> and preceding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. It came at different times in different areas and was absent in some, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The site of Belovode in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> has the world's oldest securely dated evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">copper smelting</a> at high temperature, from c. 5,000 BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Ancient Near East</a> it began in the late 5th millennium BCE and lasted about a millennium; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> it was a short period between about 2,500 and 2,200 BCE. The Hungarian scientist Ferenc Pulszky proposed the concept of a Copper Age in the 1870s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The late period saw more widespread and skilled making of copper tools, rings and bells, with a few objects of bronze and brass, before the Bronze Age cultures that followed; most scholars place the start of the <b>Chalcolithic</b> in the Longshan of 2600–2000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/longshan-culture">Longshan culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Mehrgarh is a Neolithic and <b>Chalcolithic</b> archaeological site on the Kacchi Plain of Balochistan in Pakistan, near the Bolan Pass and west of the Indus River, between the modern cities of Quetta, Kalat and Sibi.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mehrgarh-culture">Mehrgarh culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…continuity with the island’s Neolithic farmers, who resembled those of Iberia and southern France, with about 80% early European farmer and 20% western hunter-gatherer ancestry and almost no steppe-related ancestry; a 2021 study found a possible small flow of ancestry from the Italian peninsula from the <b>Chalcolithic</b> on.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Wheeler found ten domestic structures there and divided the site into three periods: a Neolithic or Neolithic-<b>Chalcolithic</b> Period I, a Megalithic Period II and an early historical Period III.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south the Ubaid gave way about 3800 BCE to the Uruk period, and in the north to the Late <b>Chalcolithic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ubaid-culture">Ubaid culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In metalworking the Vinča culture featured the earliest known example of copper smelting, though it is not usually counted as <b>Chalcolithic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vinca-culture">Vinča culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
